The ladder, each with the Go example:

```go
// O(1): hash map lookup — constant, regardless of size
m := map[string]int{...}
v := m["key"]  // one hash, one check

// O(log n): binary search — halve the search space each step
func binarySearch(arr []int, target int) int {
    lo, hi := 0, len(arr)-1
    for lo <= hi {
        mid := lo + (hi-lo)/2
        if arr[mid] == target { return mid }
        if arr[mid] < target { lo = mid + 1 } else { hi = mid - 1 }
    }
    return -1  // 1000 elements: ~10 steps (2^10 > 1000)
}

// O(n): linear scan — touch each element once
for _, v := range arr { if v == target { ... } }

// O(n log n): sorting
sort.Ints(arr)  // each of n elements placed via log-n comparison

// O(n²): nested loops — every pair
for i := range arr { for j := range arr { ... } }

// O(2^n): naive fibonacci — each call spawns two
func fibRec(n int) int { if n <= 1 { return n }; return fibRec(n-1) + fibRec(n-2) }
```

The numbers that matter: n=1000 — O(n) is 1000 ops, O(log n) is ~10, O(n²) is 1,000,000. The gap between log n and n² is the difference between instant and never.

The space companion: O(1) in-place sort, O(n) for a copy, O(log n) recursion depth. Interviewers check both.
